{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q15",
  "speechText": "For me, I would evaluate retrieval and the final answer separately. The main reason is that if retrieval gives the wrong evidence, the final LLM answer cannot be reliable even if it sounds good. For example, with a labeled test set, I can check whether the relevant document or chunk appears in the top results and compare ranking quality when the retrieval strategy changes. Because of that, after retrieval, I check whether the final answer is correct, grounded in the evidence, and avoids unsupported claims. At the same time, I also track latency, embedding failures, empty retrieval, token cost, and fallback rate. I keep a stable eval dataset and rerun it when embeddings, ranking, prompts, or models change. So overall, I evaluate the system at three levels: retrieval quality, answer quality, and production metrics so I can detect regressions clearly.",
